## 1. Coordinate system decisions
**Working CRS:** EPSG:32631 (WGS 84 / UTM zone 31N)

**Why this one:** Because I am generating a kernel density heatmap and measuring distances in Lagos State, I must use a projected coordinate system measured in meters rather than degrees.

| Dataset | CRS as downloaded | CRS after | Operation |
|---|---|---|---|
| Lagos_LGAs_Boundary | EPSG:4326 | EPSG:32631 | Reprojected 
| nga_health_facilities | EPSG:4326 | EPSG:32631 | Reprojected |

---

## 2. Spatial Extent and Clipping

- **Boundary used:** `data/raw/Lagos Health.gpkg`
- **Features before clipping:** 4657
- **Features after clipping:** <603>
The original humanitarian dataset covered all of Nigeria. I used the QGIS Intersection tool to clip the health facility points strictly to the Lagos State administrative boundary.

---

## 3. The five quality checks

| Check | Result | Action taken |
|---|---|---|
| Is the CRS what I think it is? | yes | Reprojected both the boundary and facility points to UTM Zone 31N to resolve spatial mismatch.
| Are there nulls in the fields I need? | yes | Logged missing values (1,326 missing names; 100% missing operators) in my data notes. Proceeding with density analysis based purely on location.
| Are there duplicate features? | no | Ran the 'Delete duplicate geometries' tool. 0 duplicates were found. |
| Is the geometry valid? | yes | Ran the 'Check validity' tool. 0 invalid geometries were found.
| Does coverage span the study area? | Not fully | Discovered that healthcare facility points are not  100% distributed across the Lagos LGAs after clipping.

## 4. Problems encountered and what I did.

- I realised there were some geometry error in the bounndary data that I downloaded, I had to utilise the QuickOSM vector plugin to download another Lagos State boundary. 

- I also e noticed that the symbology of the boundary data had an opacity of 100%, thus, I could not see the point data. I had to reduce the opacity to 6.7%.

